WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.373
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B two.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:09.489
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:16.308
Unit twenty-seven: Participle Clauses. Today you will shorten sentences with -ing and -ed clauses.

4
00:00:16.608 --> 00:00:19.132
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.648
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.426
Walking.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:29.090
Walking means an -ing form starting a clause.

8
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:31.648
Walking home, I met Tom.

9
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.995
Your turn. Say: Walking.

10
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:38.869
Walking.

11
00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:40.792
Having finished.

12
00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:45.315
Having finished means a perfect participle for an earlier action.

13
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:47.638
Having finished, she left.

14
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.460
Your turn. Say: Having finished.

15
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.417
Having finished.

16
00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:57.138
Built in.

17
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.405
Built in means a passive participle.

18
00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:03.469
Built in 1900, it is old.

19
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:06.678
Your turn. Say: Built in.

20
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:10.569
Built in.

21
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:12.376
Based on.

22
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:15.545
Based on means a passive participle.

23
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.778
Based on a true story.

24
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:21.814
Your turn. Say: Based on.

25
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:25.691
Based on.

26
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:27.428
Reduce.

27
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:30.161
Reduce means to make shorter.

28
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.192
Reduce the clause.

29
00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:35.424
Your turn. Say: Reduce.

30
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:39.349
Reduce.

31
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:41.363
Same subject.

32
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.487
Same subject means both clauses need the same doer.

33
00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:47.874
The same subject is needed.

34
00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.275
Your turn. Say: Same subject.

35
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:55.401
Same subject.

36
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.058
Active.

37
00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.479
Active means the subject does the action.

38
00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:02.642
An active participle.

39
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.508
Your turn. Say: Active.

40
00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:09.275
Active.

41
00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:11.006
Passive.

42
00:02:12.017 --> 00:02:14.515
Passive means the subject receives the action.

43
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:16.803
A passive participle.

44
00:02:17.417 --> 00:02:19.686
Your turn. Say: Passive.

45
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:23.349
Passive.

46
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:28.016
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.663
Visal, combine: I opened the door. I saw a letter.

48
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:45.426
Opening the door, I saw a letter. A participle clause shortens a sentence when both actions have the same subject. It sounds compact and is common in writing.

49
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:48.249
How do we choose the form?

50
00:02:49.350 --> 00:03:08.015
Active: -ing for simultaneous or close actions: Walking home, I met Tom. Perfect: having + past participle for an earlier action: Having finished, she left. Passive: -ed: Built in 1900, the bridge is strong.

51
00:03:09.117 --> 00:03:10.454
What is the danger?

52
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:25.269
The subject of the participle must match the main clause. Walking down the street, a dog bit me is wrong; it suggests the dog was walking. Write Walking down the street, I was bitten by a dog.

53
00:03:26.433 --> 00:03:28.189
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:33.766
Grammar focus: Participle clauses: -ing, having plus -ed, -ed.

55
00:03:34.066 --> 00:03:35.311
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:37.958
Opening the door, I saw a letter.

57
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.070
Having finished the report, she went home.

58
00:03:41.983 --> 00:03:45.527
Built in 1900, the bridge is still strong.

59
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:56.032
-ing = active, same time. Having + -ed = active, earlier. -ed = passive. The subject of the main clause must be the doer of the participle.

60
00:03:56.783 --> 00:04:00.806
Be careful! Don't say: Walking home, a dog bit me.

61
00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.823
Say: Walking home, I was bitten by a dog.

62
00:04:06.123 --> 00:04:07.577
Same subject needed.

63
00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:10.880
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:13.097
Shorter Sentences.

65
00:04:13.397 --> 00:04:15.687
Opening the door, I saw a letter on the floor.

66
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.350
Having finished the report, she went home.

67
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:23.517
Built in 1900, the bridge is still strong.

68
00:04:24.533 --> 00:04:27.160
Based on a true story, the film moved everyone.

69
00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:31.908
The subject of the participle must be the same as in the main clause.

70
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.897
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.645
blank the door, I saw a letter.

72
00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:43.413
A, Opening. B, Opened. C, Having open.

73
00:04:46.967 --> 00:04:48.873
The answer is A: Opening.

74
00:04:50.017 --> 00:04:52.576
blank dinner, he went out. (earlier)

75
00:04:52.876 --> 00:04:56.193
A, Having eaten. B, Eating. C, Eaten.

76
00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:01.710
The answer is A: Having eaten.

77
00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.418
blank in 1900, the bridge is old.

78
00:05:05.718 --> 00:05:08.884
A, Built. B, Building. C, Having build.

79
00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:14.444
The answer is A: Built.

80
00:05:15.583 --> 00:05:16.817
Which is correct?

81
00:05:17.117 --> 00:05:24.032
A, Walking home, a dog bit me.. B, Walking home, I was bitten by a dog.. C, Walked home, I met a dog..

82
00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:31.621
The answer is B: Walking home, I was bitten by a dog..

83
00:05:32.783 --> 00:05:35.224
According to the reading, what film is moving?

84
00:05:35.524 --> 00:05:39.909
A, one based on a true story. B, a comedy. C, a musical.

85
00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:46.697
The answer is A: one based on a true story.

86
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.644
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:52.483 --> 00:05:57.530
Combine: 'I opened the door. I saw a letter.' using a participle clause.

88
00:06:06.317 --> 00:06:07.782
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:08.082 --> 00:06:18.594
Opening the door, I saw a letter on the floor. I use opening because both actions have the same subject, and the clause is shorter and more elegant.

90
00:06:18.894 --> 00:06:20.848
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:27.711
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clauses: -ing, having plus -ed, -ed.

92
00:06:28.011 --> 00:06:33.113
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:33.413 --> 00:06:36.356
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

94
00:06:36.656 --> 00:06:40.098
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

